        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "1c0d871f95b1b60a9e869ff2918ae3b94a263826",
            "parents": [
                "5bffc3aeba5fcce359d0b92cf611df19435dc805"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/48/88248/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "D. L. Marrero",
                "email": "dmarrero@nvidia.com",
                "username": "dlmarrero"
            },
            "createdOn": 1773339873,
            "author": {
                "name": "D. L. Marrero",
                "email": "dmarrero@nvidia.com",
                "username": "dlmarrero"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "What is the advantage of opengrep over ast-grep?  Is there a reason we should choose opengrep?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "D. L. Marrero",
                        "email": "dmarrero@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "dlmarrero"
                    },
                    "message": "opengrep is more mature. There are lots of rules already out there that have been created by the community which developers could build off of. That being said, ast-grep is supposed to be faster and building it from source seems much more straightforward than building opengrep. If those aspects of ast-grep are more preferable, it shouldn't be too difficult to port the bmcweb rules to ast-grep."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "ast-grep isn't integrated in build-scripts, so while we could make a comparison, why with that one specifically?  I've used both before, and both could probably do it, opengrep is the one Davy chose (not sure of his reasons)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "My reasons for suggesting ast-grep were: popularity, stability, and easy of use of toolchain.\n\n1. Popularity: https://www.star-history.com/?repos=opengrep%2Fopengrep%2Cast-grep%2Fast-grep%2Csemgrep%2Fsemgrep&type=date&legend=top-left\n\nast-grep seems to be more popular than opengrep.  It is less popular than semgrep, but pretty similar.  The license change of semgrep is what caused opengrep and gives me some concerns for building on top of.\n\n2. Stability: semgrep being a commercial-ish product and then opengrep becoming a fork of it doesn't give me a lot of comfort in building our infrastructure around it.\n\n3. Toolchain: Rust vs OCaml ... should self explanatory."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "D. L. Marrero",
                        "email": "dmarrero@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "dlmarrero"
                    },
                    "message": "That was a good call. ast-grep flagged issues that opengrep missed due to opengrep failure to parse certain files. patchsets submitted with ast-grep instead of opengrep."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Rock on."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Do we have a sample of things we're expecting to implement?  Are there bugs you've been able to find in the openbmc source already that aren't something that would have been caught with clang-tidy?  Can you add a reference?\n\nIf we find this useful across the project, wouldn't we want project-level rules?  I'm surprised we'd want repository-specific rules."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "D. L. Marrero",
                        "email": "dmarrero@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "dlmarrero"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, there is another commit under review for bmcweb under this topic: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/q/topic:%22opengrep-ci%22\n\nThere you will find rules that are specific to the issues described in docs/COMMON_ERRORS.md. Some of those rules could probably become project-level rules, but others are specific to bmcweb."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "(Full disclosure, I asked Davy to push for better integration of this with the project, and was aware of it before the review because it came up in a security context)\n\nThere's some codification of the bmcweb coding rules attached to the same topic as this review that found some post-auth crash bugs.  Ultimately, as a maintainer think something like this would help reviews move faster.  At last scan, it found ~10 violations that got missed in code review/other static analysis.\n\n> wouldn't we want project-level rules? \n\nMaybe someday?  I can't think of a rule that we would apply universally using ast grepping, but certainly could be possible."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "What is the rationale for adding as another step in unit-test.py instead of adding as a linter/formatter in `scripts/format-code.sh`?  We already have infrastructure there for doing things like adding step-specific ignore files, adding common project-level configuration, etc."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "D. L. Marrero",
                        "email": "dmarrero@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "dlmarrero"
                    },
                    "message": "You're right, `format-code.sh` is a better place to add it. I will make that change and submit a new patchset."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Because it's not a code formatter was my thought when asked offhand.  I may have steered Davy the wrong way here.  @Davy, sorry, Patrick is probably right now looking at it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
